Counting Calories
I no longer count calories, but at one point, at least for me, it was an important skill to learn. It has since helped me develop a basic understanding of nutrition and a lot of the nuance needed to address issues related to health and fitness.


Among bodybuilders, “counting macros” is the primary nutritional approach to weight loss and body composition. The idea centers around building muscle mass. One reason building muscle mass is important for weight loss is that it helps prevent injury. While cardio is generally better for acute weight loss through immediate energy expenditure, muscle acts as a metabolic sink—it stores energy and gives incoming calories a place to go. Furthermore, for many people, building muscle is a strategy that satisfies aesthetic goals in ways that cardio alone cannot.
 
One reason I do not count calories is that I feel traditional bodybuilding culture does not do a particularly good job of recognizing and implementing strategies that promote gut health. Quite a bit of actionable gut health advice comes from academic researchers and professors who are not necessarily connected to mainstream bodybuilding. My approach to gut health revolves primarily around eating salads, foods high in resistant starch, and complex carbohydrates. Salads contain fiber and phytonutrients that support specific physiological processes in ways that go far beyond generic targets for protein, fat, and carbohydrates.
To start with the most basic building blocks, we have protein, fat, and carbohydrates:
* Protein: Essential and simple, protein is widely known for building lean tissue. Bodybuilders emphasize it because it offers a straightforward, protein-centric framework for weight loss and muscle retention. Beyond supporting tissue repair, protein intake also plays a role in structural gastrointestinal maintenance.
* Fat: Dietary fat is essential—containing mandatory nutrients like essential fatty acids—and is vital for structural cell integrity. There are trillions of cells in the human body undergoing constant turnover, with cell renewal rates ranging from a few days (such as the gut lining) to several weeks or months.
* Carbohydrates: Carbohydrates are often the most feared macronutrient. While not strictly "essential" for baseline survival in the way essential fatty acids are, carbohydrates are necessary for key metabolic adaptations. Despite getting a bad rap in popular media, literature supports carbs as a primary energy source. As an endurance athlete, I have learned that carbohydrates are non-negotiable for sustaining regional-elite running paces.
From a chemical perspective:
* 1 gram of protein = 4 calories
* 1 gram of fat = 9 calories
* 1 gram of carbohydrate = 4 calories
To count calories, you would just simply figure out how many total grams of protein, carbs and fat is in the food you eat each meal. Multiply this number by how many calories each macronutrient contains and add those totals together. 
If you add up all the totals from the foods you have eaten in a day, you will have your total caloric count for the day. In general, this is useful for also determining how much of the USRDA metrics you are meeting on a daily basis. Carbs are also split into “simple” and complex carbs and in many nutrition information labels, a “net carbs” metric is given.


So, why is this important?
To count calories, you figure out the total grams of protein, carbs, and fat in the food you consume at each meal, multiply those numbers by their respective caloric values, and sum the totals. Adding up these values across the day yields your total caloric intake.
Much of the personal training industry is built around aesthetics and weight management, where counting calories serves as a fundamental quantitative tool. A general rule suggests that our bodies rely upon "maintenance" calories to keep body weight stable. To lose weight, you would typically consume roughly 150 to 300 calories below maintenance; to gain muscle or weight, you would consume roughly 150 to 300 calories above maintenance.
This framework relies on the concept of Calories In / Calories Out (CICO), where individuals balance their daily energy intake against their Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE).
